"rovider-only redemption | Ratified 2026-10-04 (owner); activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4g, R5.4j; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4 |\\\\n+| [D-0521](#d-0521--usage-pins-protect-both-da-retention-classes) | Usage pins prevent pruning of Ephemeral and Leased blobs without extending expiry | Task #2228 implementation decision | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.3 R5.3d |\\\\n | [D-0522](#d-0522--agent-airdrop-spend-to-unlock) | Agent grant unlocks one FLOP per three FLOP of locked principal in settled `payable`s, spend capped at three quarters of principal, locked-first payout and refund; no end block | Ratified 2026-10-05 (owner); spend cap and attribution await owner confirmation in review | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7, R8.8; Appendices E.38, H.4 |\\\\n \\\\n | [D-0523](#d-0523--dormant-htlc-timeout-burn) | Default-zero HTLC burn implementation and proposed activation semantics | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification; not activated | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a710, E.48 |\\\\n \\\\n | [D-0524](#d-0524--slot-and-tranche-position-rent) | Rent rounding per slot and funding tranche, historical replacement entitlements and payer dust refunds | Ratified 2026-10-07 (owner); activation profile pending E.54 | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a75.4 R5.4e/f; Appendices E.54, F.7, G.4 |\\\\n+| [D-0525](#d-0525--inference-fee-split-85-miner--15-validator) | Inference fee settlement split: 85% miner / 15% validator, 1% audit earmark inside the validator leg | Ratified 2026-10-14 (owner) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79 R9.5, \u00a712.1 R12.1a/d; Appendices D, E.38, F.3, H.5 |\\\\n+| [D-0526](#d-0526--active-set-cap-and-babe-authority-alignment-1000-active-validators-100-finalizers) | Active-set cap and BABE authority alignment (1,000 active validators, 100 finalizers) | Ratified 2026-10-15 (closes #1393 / YP E.41) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.2, \u00a715.2, \u00a715.4, \u00a715.5, E.41 |\\\\n+| [D-0527](#d-0527--miner-airdrop-unlock-credits-miner-block-rewards) | Miner grant unlock credit counts the grantee's minted miner-leg block rewards, at finality, alongside its settled payouts; supersedes D-0517 item 1 | Ratified 2026-10-12 (owner; #2625) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a78.2 R8.7 |\\\\n+| [D-0528](#d-0528--sponsor-owned-registration-bonds-count-as-self-stake-on-devnet-and-testnet) | Sponsor-owned, stake-only registration bonds count as operator self-stake on devnet and testnet only, refunded to the sponsor; mainnet unchanged | Ratified 2026-10-17 (owner; #2676, settles #683 for devnet/testnet) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.3, \u00a76.1 R6.1a, \u00a715.2; Appendices E.32, G.3, H.1 |\\\\n+| [D-0529](#d-0529--zero-burn-across-all-protocol-paths) | Zero burn across all protocol paths: 0% transaction-fee burn (`BurnFees = 0`), 0% inference-fee burn, and 100% routing of slashes to `FLOPFoundationAccount` | Ratified 2026-09-22 (owner; #1945, #2792) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79.1 R9.6, \u00a79.3 R9.10, \u00a711.3, \u00a713.2, \u00a715.7, Appendices A, D, H |\\\\n+| [D-0530](#d-0530--operator-self-stake-only-and-duration-locked-community-staking-pool) | Prohibition of delegated staking into validators and miners (self-stake only); deprecation of delegation extrinsics and commissions; 5% duration-locked community staking pool | Ratified 2026-09-08 (owner; #2792) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a72.3, \u00a76.1, \u00a79.1 R9.12, \u00a711.3, \u00a715.2, \u00a715.5, Appendices A, C, D, E.32, E.40, G.3, H |\\\\n+| [D-0531](#d-0531--off-chain-referral-leaderboard-and-lottery-program) | Off-chain explorer referral leaderboard and periodic referral lotteries funded from `genesis_reserve` (800M FLOP), superseding on-chain referral commissions | Ratified 2026-09-23 / 2026-09-24 (owner; #1358, #2792) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a79.1 R9.4, Appendices A, E.38 |\\\\n+| [D-0532](#d-0532--miner-capacity-bond-one-unit-window-inside-the-coefficient-value-set-against-a-security-envelope) | Miner capacity bond: one unit (FLOP per G_n/s of calibrated cap), dispute window inside the coefficient, surge as a separate margin; `k` set at activation against a security envelope (0.25 candidate); amends D-0418 | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification (#1361) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a76.1, Appendices A, C.1, E.33, H |\\\\n+| [D-0533](#d-0533--model-registry-base-price-per-g_n-a-reference-set-against-emission-at-a-reference-throughput) | Model-registry base price per `G_n`: a FLOP-denominated reference set by `\u03c6\u00b7P_m(era)/T_ref`, replacing the devnet-scale 0.003; 5e-7 FLOP is the benchmark to validate, and the value is set at activation alongside D-0532 | Proposal \u2014 pending ratification (#1361 follow-up) | [Yellowpaper](../yellowpaper.md), \u00a712.1, Appendices A, E.24, E.44, E.49 |\\\\n \\\\n ---\\\\n \\\\n@@ -927,6 +937,27 @@ open as a later profile version. The finalized-prefix clock follows \u00a71.2 and \u00a7\\\\n second opinions and the implementation handoff are on #1836. Background:\\\\n economics, mechanism and security review.\\\\n \\\\n+## D-0521 \u2014 Usage pins protect both DA retention classes\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** implementation decision for task #2228 on 2026-10-02. Supersedes the Leased-only\\\\n+scope of D-0407's prune pin guard; preserves D-0441's expiry and dispute-horizon rules.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context.** `pin` accepts any available DA blob, and compute-channel pins Ephemeral transcript\\\\n+references after checking their availability through the dispute horizon. `prune_blob` previously\\\\n+ignored those pins at window end, contrary to the pin API's promise to protect in-flight usage.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision.** Both retention classes require `pin_count == 0` before pruning. Ephemeral blobs still\\\\n+require their finalized retention deadline; Leased blobs still require `lease_end + grace`. Pins\\\\n+do not extend expiry or allow new usage after expiry. Retention errors retain precedence over\\\\n+`BlobPinned`, and an expired blob becomes prunable once its final pin is released.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Specification.** \u00a75.3 R5.3d.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Rationale & sources.** A shared prune guard preserves the existing pin interface and transcript\\\\n+callers. Rejecting Ephemeral pins would require changing those callers and their protection\\\\n+contract. The DA Veil model proves the guard for both classes and checks that an expired Ephemeral\\\\n+blob cannot be pruned while pinned, then can be pruned after unpinning.\\\\n+\\\\n ## D-0522 \u2014 Agent airdrop spend-to-unlock\\\\n \\\\n **Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-10-05. The owner's instruction in the session was: fix\\\\n@@ -1074,3 +1105,482 @@ proves the term bound; `slot_interval_fairness` proves the strict one-unit inter\\\\n `PaidStorageSettlement.lean` already indexes intervals by position. These are specification/model\\\\n contracts, not runtime conformance receipts. #1837 must consume this decision and the regression\\\\n vectors before its payout implementation is ready. This task changes no pallet code or launch default.\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0525 \u2014 Inference fee split (85% miner / 15% validator)\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified 2026-10-14 (owner; #2702). Brought into line across yellow paper \u00a79, \u00a712.1,\\\\n+Appendices D, E.38, F.3, H.5 and canonical parameters.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context.** Executive ruling by Shu on 2026-10-14 confirms that inference fees in compute channels\\\\n+are split 85% to the miner and 15% to validators. This represents the ratified economics in the\\\\n+tokenomics projection model (`FLOP Tokenomics & Airdrop.xlsx`, Projection Input), institutional\\\\n+counterparty agreements (Pantera, Gaorong/HashKey, Luganodes, BitGo, Caladan, Kraken), and the\\\\n+published teaser. Previously, the Yellowpaper spec stated in Appendix D that settlement had \\\\\\\"no settlement fee\\\\\\\"\\\\n+(100% to miner), and \u00a79 R9.5 forbade increasing the validator pool with an inference surcharge.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Supersedes by scope.** Supersedes D-0403's preliminary framing where the only deduction from a session\\\\n+payment was the 1% audit earmark against 99% miner payment.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision.**\\\\n+1. **Settlement fee split:** Every settled compute-channel session payment (`payable` at `settle`,\\\\n+   `certificate_settle`, or `force_settle \u2192 finalize`) is split **85% to the miner**\\\\n+   ([`inference_fee_miner_share_ppt`](../yellowpaper.md#param-inference_fee_miner_share_ppt) = 850 ppt) and **15% to validators**\\\\n+   ([`inference_fee_validator_share_ppt`](../yellowpaper.md#param-inference_fee_validator_share_ppt) = 150 ppt).\\\\n+2. **Audit fee earmark inside the validator leg:** The 1% audit pool earmark\\\\n+   ([`audit_fee_split_ppm`](../yellowpaper.md#param-audit_fee_split_ppm) = 10,000 ppm) sits *inside* the 15% validator leg,\\\\n+   funding the pool that pays `claim_audit_fee`. The remaining 14% is distributed across active\\\\n+   validators according to the \u00a79 R9.5 committee-premium weighting (1.1\u00d7 for finality committee members)\\\\n+   and is liquid on issue per D-0518.\\\\n+3. **Scope of R9.5:** Reword R9.5 to clarify that the prohibition on surcharges and second mints applies\\\\n+   to the *block-reward pool* (10% emission leg stays 10%). The 15% inference fee leg is an extrinsic\\\\n+   settlement fee, not an emission or dilution.\\\\n+4. **Self-dealing residual in E.38:** Re-derive the self-dealing recycling analysis: an operator\\\\n+   buying inference from itself recovers ~85% of escrow (after the 15% validator deduction), meaning\\\\n+   unlocking locked principal via self-inference incurs a ~15% economic cost, not ~0.75%.\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0526 \u2014 Active-set cap and BABE authority alignment (1,000 active validators, 100 finalizers)\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified 2026-10-15 (closes #1393 / YP E.41).\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context.** D-0437 (#1400) ratified `validator_active_set_cap = 1,000` in the yellowpaper and protocol\\\\n+parameters, but left open the authority-set sizing question (YP stub E.41). In the runtime,\\\\n+`MAX_ACTIVE_VALIDATORS` and `MaxAuthorities` were set to 200, while `ValidatorSessionManager::new_session`\\\\n+passed the entire active set to `pallet_session` untruncated. Any active set above 200 would have collided\\\\n+with the BABE authority bound. Additionally, `ActiveValidators` storage was bounded by `ConstU32<10_000>`,\\\\n+which mismatched the ratified 1,000 active set cap.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision.**\\\\n+1. **Option (a) ratified:** Raise `MAX_ACTIVE_VALIDATORS` and `MaxAuthorities` to 1,000. Every active\\\\n+   validator is a BABE block authoring authority. Because BABE slot evaluation evaluates a private VRF\\\\n+   locally per slot with zero gossip round overhead, 1,000 authorities incur no interactive consensus\\\\n+   round messaging.\\\\n+2. **AlephBFT finality decoupled:** AlephBFT finality remains strictly bounded to the 100-member PoUI-gated\\\\n+   committee ([`finality_committee_size`](../yellowpaper.md#param-finality_committee_size) = 100, `AlephMaxCommittee` = 100).\\\\n+   The networking unit-gossip peer budget `ALEPH_SET_PEERS` in `pallet-relay-node` is sized to\\\\n+   `AlephMaxCommittee::get() + ALEPH_RELAY_HEADROOM` (150 peers) rather than 1,000, preventing redundant\\\\n+   gossip mesh allocations.\\\\n+3. **Storage bound alignment:** Align `ActiveValidators` storage bound from `ConstU32<10_000>` to\\\\n+   `ConstU32<1_000>`, verified with a decode-compatibility test against prior SCALE encodings.\\\\n+   `ValidatorQueue` preserves its registration capacity (`ConstU32<10_000>`) per yellowpaper \u00a715.5 R15.5b.\\\\n+4. **Promotion enforcement:** When `ActiveValidators.len() == ActiveSetCap` (1,000), `rotate` cleanly\\\\n+   refuses promotion without panic or storage overflow, preserving candidate order in `ValidatorQueue`.\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0527 \u2014 Miner airdrop unlock credits miner block rewards\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified by the owner. The ruling is recorded in the body of #2625 (2026-10-12) and in\\\\n+\u00a71 item 2 of outstanding-decisions, not in a review signature.\\\\n+Supersedes D-0517 item 1's exclusion of block\\\\n+rewards and the block-reward half of D-0517's rationale. The rest of D-0517 stands: a quarter at the\\\\n+start block, no end block, the payout credit and its dispute-window rule, locked Miner principal usable\\\\n+only as self-stake, slash attribution, and the Validator release. This documentation changes no\\\\n+runtime behavior; the implementation is #1175.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context.** D-0517 item 1 credited a Miner grant only for the payouts its grantee receives as a\\\\n+channel's miner. The terms the team gives counterparties count block rewards as well, and the\\\\n+projection model's estimate of about four months to unlock at constant capacity holds only if they\\\\n+count. The public airdrop page, generated from D-0517, gave a counterparty the opposite answer.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision.**\\\\n+\\\\n+1. A Miner grant's unlock credit accrues one FLOP per FLOP of either receipt:\\\\n+   1. the payout the grantee receives as the channel's miner, as D-0517 item 1 defines it and counted\\\\n+      as it says: once the settlement is finalized and the channel dispute window has passed with no\\\\n+      upheld fraud dispute;\\\\n+   2. the grantee's share of the miner leg of the block reward (`miner_share_ppt`, 75% of each block\\\\n+      reward) as actually minted to the grantee account in a block-reward distribution, counted once\\\\n+      the distribution block is finalized.\\\\n+2. No other receipt counts: the validator, agent and staker legs of the block reward, the\\\\n+   Labs/Foundation subsidy, transaction fees and tips, forward-availability payments, refunds,\\\\n+   penalties and dispute bonds. Each receipt counts once.\\\\n+3. The unlocked amount stays `min(P \u2212 S, \u230aP/4\u230b + credit)` with no end block. Whether a fraud verdict\\\\n+   upheld later reverses credit of either kind follows E.44.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Specification.** \u00a78.2 R8.7.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Rationale & sources.** D-0517 gave two reasons to exclude block rewards. Both still describe the\\\\n+mechanism: the owner accepts the first, and the second is bounded by what the miner leg pays for.\\\\n+\\\\n+- *A clock.* The era-0 miner leg is 72 FLOP per block, about 6.22M FLOP a day. If the airdropped\\\\n+  miners earned all of it, block rewards alone would unlock the 900M locked tranche in about 145 days\\\\n+  whatever the demand; at the miner calculator's launch share (1,000 of 1,200 H100-equivalents) they\\\\n+  take about 174 days. The published \\\\\\\"about four months\\\\\\\" is this constant-capacity case with payouts\\\\n+  added. In the calculator's base case the network grows past the airdropped cohort, its share falls,\\\\n+  and the whole tranche unlocks by month 8. It is not a calendar clock: a grantee that stops doing\\\\n+  verified work stops unlocking, and its share of the leg falls as other miners' verified `G_n` grows.\\\\n+- *The synthetic-task floor.* `pallet_block_rewards` splits the miner leg pro rata to each miner's\\\\n+  `G_n` for the distribution period (`PeriodGn`), and its only writer is `pallet_flop_poui`'s\\\\n+  `OnProofVerified`: quorum-attested direct-rail proofs (synthetic and ghost, calibration) under R3.6b.\\\\n+  The simulator path that skips the quorum is compiled out of production runtimes. Block-reward credit\\\\n+  is therefore credit for verified capacity, including the synthetic-task bootstrap floor (\u00a79.2), and\\\\n+  never for registered but idle hardware. Limiting the credit to that leg is the filter; no separate\\\\n+  one is needed. Minted rewards are liquid (R9.13) and nothing claws back credited `G_n` or a minted\\\\n+  reward, so credit counts at finality with no dispute-window lag. A future direct-rail clawback would\\\\n+  need a matching lag.\\\\n+- *Self-dealing.* Current-profile channel payout issues no public work credit and does not feed\\\\n+  `BlockRewards` (R11.2b), so wash demand through an agent the miner controls buys no block-reward\\\\n+  credit. It still buys payout credit, at the 15% validator-leg cost E.38 records (D-0525). If a later\\\\n+  profile lets channel payout earn public work credit (E.44), wash demand would also raise a grantee's\\\\n+  share of the miner leg; that case belongs to E.49. A validator leader grading ghost tasks for a\\\\n+  miner it controls is threat V8 and is unchanged.\\\\n+\\\\n+The validator leg stays excluded because a Validator grant has its own release rule. Transaction fees\\\\n+stay excluded under either realization of R9.6: the interim one that pays the block author, and the\\\\n+ratified target that attributes the miner share by `G_n`.\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0528 \u2014 Sponsor-owned registration bonds count as self-stake on devnet and testnet\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified by the owner on 2026-10-17 for devnet and testnet, in the brief for #2676. It settles\\\\n+#683 for those networks only; mainnet treatment stays open in E.32. #1361 (the capacity formula) also\\\\n+stays open, so grants are sized from the live on-chain requirement, never from a constant. SPEC-024\\\\n+positions keep their mainnet meaning.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context.** Outside GPU miners began onboarding on devnet-ts on 2026-10-17. Each one needed the core team\\\\n+to transfer about 0.6\u20131M FLOP (a miner) or the 1.2M FLOP validator minimum (D-0440). A plain transfer is\\\\n+spendable: an agent could pose as a miner or validator and spend the grant on inference. SPEC-024 sponsor\\\\n+positions already kept principal in the sponsor account, but only as backing: they never satisfied the\\\\n+miner capacity bond or the validator floor, and they required an operator that had already registered\\\\n+with its own funds.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision.**\\\\n+\\\\n+1. On devnet and testnet runtimes, active sponsored principal counts as the operator's self-stake:\\\\n+   toward the miner capacity bond (`required_self_stake_for`, `is_capacity_bonded`, the hw-calibration\\\\n+   bond check and the audit-forfeit base) and toward the validator effective minimum (queue promotion and\\\\n+   `rejoin`). On mainnet, sponsored stake stays backing and never satisfies self-stake. A build feature\\\\n+   (`sponsored-self-stake`, implied by the devnet `volta` build and by the `testnet` build) selects the\\\\n+   rule; on its own it is a compile error, and CI checks that the mainnet node build reports it off.\\\\n+   Governance cannot switch it.\\\\n+2. The bond is sponsor-owned and usable only as stake. It stays frozen in the sponsor account, so the\\\\n+   operator can never transfer it, pay fees with it, place it on hold or escrow, or back other stake with\\\\n+   it. It is slashable while active or unbonding, and it is refunded only to the sponsor, after the role's\\\\n+   unbonding window has elapsed on the finalized prefix. The slash-proceeds account may not post\\\\n+   principal that counts as self-stake, because slashing it would pay that account back. While it still\\\\n+   holds a live position from before the rule was enabled, no sponsored principal is credited at all\\\\n+   (fail closed) until that position is withdrawn. The bond stays at risk for slashing and exit.\\\\n+   Sponsor principal is the sponsor account's only staking freeze: an account with own stake, a\\\\n+   delegation or a freeze in the other staking pallet cannot sponsor, and a sponsor account cannot\\\\n+   self-stake or delegate in either pallet. When a running chain first enables the rule, positions\\\\n+   whose sponsor already holds another staking freeze are quarantined, and nothing is credited until\\\\n+   that scan finishes (it runs in bounded per-block batches) or while any quarantined position\\\\n+   remains live (fail closed).\\\\n+3. A sponsor may register an operator that holds no balance (`sponsor_register_miner`,\\\\n+   `sponsor_register_validator`), posting the whole bond. The bond must cover the live requirement:\\\\n+   `required_self_stake_for(miner)` for a miner, `effective_minimum_stake()` for a validator.\\\\n+   `sponsor_top_up` grows an existing position when the requirement rises.\\\\n+4. On devnet and testnet an operator's deregistration starts the unbonding of its active sponsor\\\\n+   positions, and the account cannot register again until they are withdrawn: a slash of the new\\\\n+   registration must never reach sponsors of the old one. The operator may withdraw them after the\\\\n+   finalized unlock, and may fully unbond a position that outlived its registration, so an absent\\\\n+   sponsor cannot lock it out. Because a sponsor may unbond while a validator is jailed, `un_jail`\\\\n+   first restores the bonded self-stake to the effective minimum from the validator's own spare\\\\n+   balance, as `rejoin` does. A sponsor can release its own position with `sponsor_unbond` and then\\\\n+   `sponsor_withdraw`. If that takes an active validator below the effective minimum, the validator\\\\n+   goes back to the queue, and the unbond waits while that would cross the minimum active set; an\\\\n+   author never keeps its seat without collateral.\\\\n+5. The validator self-stake ratio for native delegation counts operator-owned stake only, so a fully\\\\n+   sponsored validator accepts no native delegation.\\\\n+6. Chain state and events report own stake (`self_stake`, zero when fully sponsored) separately from\\\\n+   sponsored stake. A sponsored bond creates no allocation right (R8.4).\\\\n+\\\\n+**Specification.** \u00a76.1 R6.1a; \u00a72.3; \u00a715.2; Appendices E.32, G.3 and H.1.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Rationale & sources.**\\\\n+\\\\n+- *Ownership instead of a lock on the operator.* Freezes and fungible holds overlap in FRAME, so frozen\\\\n+  funds in the operator's account could still be consumed by inference escrow. Keeping the principal in\\\\n+  the sponsor account closes every operator path at once and reuses the SPEC-024 slash and refund\\\\n+  machinery unchanged.\\\\n+- *One staking freeze per sponsor.* An account's frozen amount is its largest freeze, not their sum. A\\\\n+  sponsor backing a miner and a validator with the same funds would pass any admission check that sums\\\\n+  freezes, and could then transfer the smaller bond away (#2770 review). Exclusivity closes that without\\\\n+  a storage change, a shared freeze id or a move to holds.\\\\n+- *All sponsored stake counts.* A separate position kind for registration bonds would change a stored type\\\\n+  and need a migration, with no devnet or testnet behavior that depends on the distinction.\\\\n+- *Mainnet authority unchanged.* Item 4 applies only where the rule is enabled, so on mainnet only the\\\\n+  sponsor can start a refund (E.32).\\\\n+- *A dedicated build feature.* Tying the rule to `volta` alone would make a testnet build enable\\\\n+  simulator-mode crediting to get it.\\\\n+- *Residual risks, recorded in E.32 and H.1.*\\\\n+  - A sponsor's own fungible hold over its frozen principal would make a later slash transfer fail. The\\\\n+    slash then fails atomically, and so does the dispatch that needs it: a channel `finalize` that\\\\n+    forfeits audit stake cannot complete until the hold is released. The same gap applies to self-stake\\\\n+    and delegator freezes, so the sponsor service uses dedicated accounts that do nothing else.\\\\n+  - The operator still needs a small, spendable balance for the existential deposit, fees and session\\\\n+    keys.\\\\n+  - A sponsor account holds at most 64 live positions.\\\\n+  - Rotation or slash ejection leaves positions active, and the sponsor unbonds them.\\\\n+  - A validator offence reported after deregistration cannot reach its unbonding positions, as it cannot\\\\n+    reach a validator's own stake today.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Alternatives rejected.**\\\\n+\\\\n+- A plain transfer: the operator could spend it.\\\\n+- Transfer plus a freeze in the operator account: holds and fees could consume it.\\\\n+- A position-kind marker: a storage migration for no behavioral gain.\\\\n+- Moving all sponsor principal into a pallet escrow account: it changes the balance model of every\\\\n+  existing position.\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0529 \u2014 Zero burn across all protocol paths\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified by the owner (executive ruling by Shu, 2026-09-22; #1945, #2792). Supersedes the interim 10% transaction-fee burn in \u00a79.1 R9.6 and the 80/10/10 transaction-fee burn target, and replaces \\\\\\\"full-burn\\\\\\\" terminology on slash paths (`D-0409`, `D-0420`).\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context:** Earlier drafts of \u00a79.1 R9.6 and `params/flop-protocol-params.yaml` retained an interim 10% transaction-fee burn (`tx_fee_burn_percent_live = 10`, `BurnFees = Percent::from_percent(10)`) and described 100% fraud/equivocation slashes in \u00a711.3, \u00a713.2, and \u00a715.7 as \\\\\\\"100% burn\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"full-burn fault class\\\\\\\", even though R9.10 and the runtime already transferred slash proceeds to `FLOPFoundationAccount`.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision:**\\\\n+\\\\n+1. **Zero transaction-fee burn:** `tx_fee_burn_percent_live` is `0%` (`BurnFees = Percent::from_percent(0)`). `tx_fee_author_share_percent_live` is `100%` (`AllFeesToAuthor = Percent::from_percent(100)`): 100% of the live base, encoded-length, and adjusted-weight transaction inclusion fee and 100% of tips route to the block author.\\\\n+2. **Zero inference-fee burn:** Session settlement fees carry 0% burn and no platform take, splitting 85% to the compute miner and 15% to the validator pool (including the 1% audit pool earmark per D-0525).\\\\n+3. **Zero slash burn (100% Foundation routing):** No slashed collateral or failed-session penalty is burned across any validator or miner fault path. 100% of slashed stakes route to the protocol-derived `FLOPFoundationAccount` (\u00a79.3 R9.10). Fault classes with a 100% penalty (`slash_fraud_percent = 100%`) are full-slash fault classes routed to `FLOPFoundationAccount`.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Specification:** \u00a79.1 R9.6, \u00a79.3 R9.10, \u00a711.3, \u00a713.2, \u00a715.7; Appendices A, D, H; `params/flop-protocol-params.yaml` (`tx_fee_burn_percent_live`, `tx_fee_author_share_percent_live`, `slash_fraud_percent`); `pallets/runtime/zkverify/src/payout_conf.rs`.\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0530 \u2014 Operator self-stake only and duration-locked community staking pool\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified by the owner (executive ruling by Shu, 2026-09-08; #2792). Supersedes third-party operator delegation and commission provisions in `D-0408` and \u00a76.1 / \u00a711.3 / \u00a715.2.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context:** The v0.4/v0.5 draft inherited third-party stake delegation into validators and miners (`pallet_validators::delegate`, `pallet_miner_staking::delegate`, `max_delegators_per_miner = 10,000`, `miner_commission_cap_percent = 20%`, `register_miner(commission)`, and `pallet_delegation_cap`). Allowing passive holders to back an operator's position for a share of its rewards creates transferable/custodied third-party operator positions and exposes non-operating holders to operator slashing waterfalls.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision:**\\\\n+\\\\n+1. **Operator self-stake only:** Validator stake (the 1,200,000 FLOP bond floor, [`validator_min_stake`](../yellowpaper.md#param-validator_min_stake)) and miner stake (the capacity-proportional self-stake requirement of \u00a76.1) are **self-stake only**. A FLOP holder cannot delegate to or back a validator's or miner's stake for a share of its rewards, and no such third-party position exists to buy, sell, or custody.\\\\n+2. **Deprecation of operator delegation and commissions:** Third-party delegation extrinsics (`miner_staking::delegate`, `miner_staking::set_commission`, `validators::delegate`), delegation caps (`max_delegators_per_miner`, `pallet_delegation_cap`), operator commission parameters (`miner_commission_cap_percent`, the `commission` argument of `miner_staking::register_miner`), and third-party delegator loss waterfalls are deprecated and superseded. Operator rewards route directly to operator accounts without commission cuts.\\\\n+3. **Duration-locked community staking pool (5% block-reward leg):** FLOP holders who do not operate hardware participate exclusively through the **duration-locked community staking pool**, which shares the [`staker_share_ppt`](../yellowpaper.md#param-staker_share_ppt) = 5% (50 ppt) block-reward allocation across seven lock duration tiers with **zero slashing risk** and no validator or miner intermediary. Its security contribution is circulating supply lockup; exact tier weights and claim distribution mechanics remain tracked under E.40 (\u00a79.1 R9.12).\\\\n+\\\\n+**Specification:** \u00a72.3, \u00a76.1, \u00a79.1 R9.12, \u00a711.3, \u00a715.2, \u00a715.5; Appendices A, C.1, D, E.32, E.40, G.3, H; `params/flop-protocol-params.yaml` (`miner_commission_cap_percent`, `max_delegators_per_miner`, `staker_share_ppt`).\\\\n+\\\\n+---\\\\n+\\\\n+## D-0531 \u2014 Off-chain referral leaderboard and lottery program\\\\n+\\\\n+**Status:** ratified by the owner (executive ruling by Shu, 2026-09-23; Arthur, 2026-09-24; #1358, #2792). Closes #1358.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Context:** Earlier tokenomics workbook drafts proposed an on-chain referral commission structure taking 6\u201310% of a referred miner's inference earnings and 10% of a referred agent's spend. On-chain fee rebates and multi-tier referral cuts distort settlement economics and invite self-referral wash loops.\\\\n+\\\\n+**Decision:**\\\\n+\\\\n+1. **No on-chain referral commissions:** The legacy 6\u201310% miner referral commission and 10% agent-spend referral commission are superseded. The protocol takes no referral commission cut from inference settlements or block rewards, and referrals are strictly single-tier (validators cannot be referred).\\\\n+2. **Public explorer leaderboard & periodic referral lotteries:** Creators register with a verified email, a FLOP wallet, and verified social handles (X, Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, or Naver) to receive a unique referral link. The network explorer publishes a public leaderboard tracking referred wallets created, total FLOP held, and period-on-period changes in FLOP held, inference paid, and HTLC escrow paid (ranked on network usage during testnet and net change in FLOP held at mainnet). Leading creators receive periodic payouts proportional to their cohort share of FLOP held, and a fixed number of referred wallets are drawn at random each period for equal lottery prizes.\\\\n+3. **Ecosystem reserve funding:** Both creator leaderboard payouts and periodic referral lotteries are funded from the 800,000,000 FLOP [`genesis_reserve`](../yellowpaper.md#param-genesis_reserve) inside the genesis pool (\u00a79.1 R9.4, alongside builder programmes and security-disclosure rewards) and settled off-chain to verified wallets.\\\\n+"
